//? Title: Minimum-degree elimination
//? Subtitle: Actual fill edges, deterministic ties and the original arrow graph
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Eliminating a variable from a sparse symmetric matrix can couple previously
uncoupled neighbors and create new nonzeros. We trace those new graph edges,
compare natural and minimum-current-degree orders on the original arrow example,
and explain why a local degree heuristic does not certify globally minimum fill.
All symbolic decisions come from the canonical elimination producer.
)

\toc

> Elimination is a clique operation

For a symmetric positive-definite matrix, eliminating vertex v couples its
remaining neighbors through the Schur complement. The symbolic graph therefore
completes that neighbor set to a clique and removes v. This predicts potential
nonzeros; numeric cancellations can make an actual entry zero. With d live
neighbors there are d(d−1)/2 pair trials, but only previously missing edges are
fill.

Minimum degree chooses the smallest live degree and breaks ties by the smallest
vertex ID. It bounds immediate pair trials locally. Existing edges, future
eliminations and ties affect total fill, so minimum degree does not minimize
either immediate missing edges or global fill exactly.

> A three-leaf star by hand

The small graph has center0 and leaves1,2,3. Natural order eliminates0 first,
inserting all three leaf-to-leaf edges. Minimum degree first chooses1, then2,
then0, then3, creating no fill. The diagram uses the actual first natural-order
fill records. A four-node example is sufficient to see why eliminating a dense
center can be costly.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/degree_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/minimum_degree.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto a = dm::algorithms::minimum_degree_arrow(4);
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::elimination_trace(a,
                                          dm::algorithms::elimination_natural_order(4));
    auto g = dm::algorithms::minimum_degree_trace(a);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("v", {0, 3},
           std::format("Actual first elimination\nvertex {} / degree {}",
                       t.steps.front().vertex, t.steps.front().neighbors.size()));
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.steps.front().fill.size(); ++k) {
        auto[u, v] = t.steps.front().fill[k];
        auto id = std::to_string(k);
        d.node(id, {7, double(k) * 3},
               std::format("Actual new fill edge\n{} -- {}", u, v)).edge(
            "v", id);
    }
    std::print("Natural fill: {}. Greedy fill: {}.\n\n| step | natural vertex | natural new fill | greedy " "vertex | greedy new fill |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n",
               t.fill_edges, g.fill_edges);
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.order.size(); ++k)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", k, t.order[k],
                   t.steps[k].fill.size(), g.order[k], g.steps[k].fill.size());
    d.save_svg("build/degree_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Natural fill: 3. Greedy fill: 0.

| step | natural vertex | natural new fill | greedy vertex | greedy new fill |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 3 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 1 | 0 | 2 | 0 |
| 2 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 3 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 |
\image(figures/degree_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual first natural elimination inserts three edges.)
\alttext(Three new leaf pairs are emitted by the producer; the greedy order inserts none.)

> Retain the original sixteen-vertex arrow

The original example has center0 adjacent to fifteen leaves. Natural order
inserts all105 missing leaf pairs on its first step. Minimum degree removes
leaves and creates zero fill. In exact symbolic Cholesky, counting the diagonal,
these orders predict136 and31 factor entries respectively: n plus the original
undirected edge count plus fill. This identity counts structural entries, not
numerical flops or measured storage bytes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/degree_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/minimum_degree.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto a = dm::algorithms::minimum_degree_arrow();
    auto natural =
        dm::algorithms::elimination_trace(a,
                                          dm::algorithms::elimination_natural_order(16));
    auto greedy = dm::algorithms::minimum_degree_trace(a);
    std::print("| original order | actual fill edges | symbolic factor entries incl diagonal |\n| --- | " "---: | ---: |\n| natural | {} | {} |\n| minimum degree | {} | {} |\n\nActual greedy " "permutation:",
               natural.fill_edges, 31 + natural.fill_edges, greedy.fill_edges,
               31 + greedy.fill_edges);
    for (auto v : greedy.order)
        std::print(" {}", v);
    std::print(".\n");
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(natural.fill_edges),
                                      double(greedy.fill_edges)},
                                     {"natural", "minimum degree"}, "new edges");
    p.title("Original 16-vertex arrow: actual fill").x_label("elimination order").y_label("new undirected edges").save_svg("build/degree_original.svg");
}
```
