> Check a closed form across graph sizes

For an n-vertex arrow, natural order inserts(n−1)(n−2)/2 edges when n is at
least2, and zero for smaller graphs. Greedy minimum degree creates none for
every size. The prescribed finite sweep checks those exact claims using the
library's own generator and actual fill counts. It does not establish a
universal advantage on arbitrary graphs.

The simple implementation scans all vertices for each choice and uses ordered
sets for clique insertion. A coarse worst-case bound is O(n cubed log(n+1)) work
and O(n squared) driver storage. This is the explicit graph heuristic, not an
approximate minimum-degree quotient-graph implementation. The trace additionally
stores every admitted input row/reference, order ID, step, live-neighbor ID and
new fill edge. Default cap is1000000 entries; zero disables it. Admission throws
before exceeding the cap and returns no partial trace.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/degree_scaling.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/minimum_degree.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, natural, greedy;
    std::print("| vertices | natural actual fill | natural formula | greedy actual fill |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 16, 24, 32}) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::minimum_degree_arrow(n);
        auto t =
            dm::algorithms::elimination_trace(a,
                                              dm::algorithms::elimination_natural_order(n));
        auto g = dm::algorithms::minimum_degree_trace(a);
        int formula = n >= 2 ? (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 2 : 0;
        x.push_back(n);
        natural.push_back(t.fill_edges);
        greedy.push_back(g.fill_edges);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, t.fill_edges, formula,
                   g.fill_edges);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, natural, "actual natural fill", {220, 38, 38}, 6).points(
        x, greedy, "actual greedy fill", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Arrow family: observed fill versus exact formula").x_label("vertices").y_label("new undirected edges").save_svg("build/degree_scaling.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| vertices | natural actual fill | natural formula | greedy actual fill |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 3 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 4 | 3 | 3 | 0 |
| 8 | 21 | 21 | 0 |
| 16 | 105 | 105 | 0 |
| 24 | 253 | 253 | 0 |
| 32 | 465 | 465 | 0 |
\image(figures/degree_scaling.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The finite arrow sweep agrees with the exact fill formula.)
\alttext(Natural fill grows quadratically; minimum-degree fill is zero for this family.)

> Contracts, conclusion and references

The graph API rejects out-of-range vertex IDs. It ignores loops and duplicate
references and interprets asymmetric lists as their symmetric union. Fixed-order
analysis requires a complete permutation. Empty graphs are valid. Results own
the original lists, including raw duplicates, plus actual decisions. The
ordinary ordering preserves legacy choices on symmetric valid graphs. No numeric
matrix, definiteness test or cancellation analysis is performed.

The small star and original arrow explain the observed savings through actual
fill events. They support this graph-family comparison while leaving global
minimum-fill and runtime claims open.

[Tinney and Walker, Direct solutions of sparse network equations by optimally ordered triangular factorization (1967)|https://doi.org/10.1109/PROC.1967.6011]
gives historical sparse-ordering context. The explicit set implementation and
smallest-ID tie rule describe this checkout.

